#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""drift-compliance — SKELETON: audit whether sessions followed the rules.

WHY this check exists
---------------------
Operational rules written in AGENTS.md/DECISIONS.md decay the moment
they're prose: everyone agrees with them, nobody can prove they were
followed. This checker turns rules into *auditable claims* — after each
session (or on demand) it replays the session's transcript/tool-calls
against ``rules.yaml`` and writes one verdict per rule to an
append-only ``compliance.jsonl`` ledger.

It is deliberately **fail-open**: a compliance bug must never break or
block a session. Worst case is a wrong UNVERIFIABLE/REVIEW row — honest
uncertainty beats a false VIOLATION that trains people to ignore the
ledger.

Rule structure (``rules.yaml``)::

    rules:
      - id: R2                              # stable id, referenced in reports
        name: "no --admin merges"           # human label
        severity: high                      # critical|high|medium|low
        source: "AGENTS.md#autonomia"       # where the rule is declared
        check_type: deterministic           # see dispatch below
        validated_against: ['session-a', 'session-b']  # sessions that
        #   proved the check works; [] => UNVALIDATED (does not fire)
        validation_deadline: "2026-12-14"    # escalate if still unvalidated
        on_deadline_miss: "escalate to owner"
        evidence: [transcript, git-guard]   # what the check may read
        pattern: "gh\\s+pr\\s+merge.*--admin"  # deterministic only
        exempt_pattern: "rules\\.yaml|check_type"  # lines that quote the
        #   rule itself must not self-trigger (systemic FP otherwise)
        negative_pattern: "--dry-run"       # same-line exemption only —
        #   a session-wide exemption once hid every real violation
        enforcement: "what the violation means / how to comply"
        note: "semantic caveats for reviewers"

check_type dispatch:

- ``deterministic`` — regex ``pattern`` over the transcript; first
  non-exempt match = VIOLATION. ``final_only: true`` restricts to the
  tail after the last user message (closing-claim rules).
- ``claims``        — negative claims ("I verified X") require a
  ``verify_pattern`` hit in a preceding char window, else VIOLATION.
- ``hybrid``        — deterministic pattern; if it fires -> VIOLATION,
  else REVIEW/UNVERIFIABLE (semantic judgment needed).
- ``custom``        — a Python function in CUSTOM_CHECKS (sequence
  analysis, cross-ledger evidence — what regex can't express).
- ``manual``        — not auto-auditable; always UNVERIFIABLE.

Statuses written per rule::

    VIOLATION | CLEAN | UNVERIFIABLE | REVIEW | UNVALIDATED | DEADLINE_MISSED

validated_against is the anti-assumption gate: a check written against a
*hypothesis* about the transcript format doesn't fire until it has been
run against real sessions with zero false positives. ``--validate RID
--against s1,s2`` does that and back-fills the field.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

